# Patch:        020-cfg-hide-row-iterators
# Phase:        pre
# Target:       matrix/src/lib.rs, matrix/src/dense.rs, matrix/src/bitrev.rs, matrix/src/row_index_mapped.rs, matrix/src/horizontally_truncated.rs, matrix/src/stack.rs, matrix/tests/pre_patch_020.rs
# Hunks:        62
# Class:        cfg-hide
# Gap:          docs/extractor-issues.md 10 (opaque return types of trait
#               methods) and 9 (the field trait hierarchy).
# Cost:         none in a normal build: every change is behind
#               cfg(hax_lean_matrix), which only p3-matrix's extraction sets.
#               In the extracted variant, the matrix trait keeps only the
#               shape, element access and strided view.
# Upstreamable: no: it exists only for the extractor.
# Drop when:    aeneas translates trait methods returning `impl Trait`, and
#               p3-field's trait hierarchy extracts.
# Test:         test p3-matrix --test pre_patch_020; lint p3-matrix unexpected_cfgs
# Mutation:     the interpolation module is gated on the cfg instead of its
#               negation, so a normal build lacks it.
#
# The matrix trait returns opaque iterators and slices from its row methods.
#
# Rust desugars each into an associated type generic over a lifetime.
#
# Aeneas stops on the trait declaration, so nothing that names the trait extracts.
#
# Hiding those methods and their overrides keeps what a view is defined by: its shape and its entries.
#
# The default body of the unchecked read is hidden too, since every impl in the crate overrides it.
#
# The extracted variant then also hides the dot products, the packed rows, the flattened view and interpolation.
#
# An attribute keeps that variant free of unused-import warnings.
#
# Tested:     test-pre-patches.py: 6715 of 6769 upstream tests pass as
#             shipped, 6715 with the pre-patches; 0 divergences from this patch.
#
